#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124316 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36c5224a-eccc-5ea9-8719-2b0dc2ddb44d" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Cylinder"}] :question/id #uuid "0cb849bd-8a9f-5305-b086-62da1a10e54f" :question/math-academy-id "e-8226" :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\dfrac{x^2}{9} + (y+1)^2 = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our elliptic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{9} + (y+1)^2 = 1, \\qquad z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k \\in \\mathbb{R}$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\left\\{(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 : \\dfrac{x^2}{9} + (y+1)^2 = 1\\right\\}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n\\left\\{ (x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2 : \\dfrac{x^2}{3^2} + \\dfrac{(y+1)^2}{1^2} = 1\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse centered at $(0,-1)$ with semi-axes of lengths $3$ and $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124317 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbeda26-2fc0-5195-be26-ae79c72fc7bf" :knowledge-point/title "Traces of Cylinders"}] :question/id #uuid "64fc4653-a2ba-5013-8c70-1f4f14e5f8be" :question/math-academy-id "e-8227" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\dfrac {x^2} 2 +  \\dfrac {(y-2)^2}4  = 1?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4 \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =2\\pm 2 \\\\ y & =0,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of two lines $y=0$ and $y=4$ in the $yz$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124746 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36c5224a-eccc-5ea9-8719-2b0dc2ddb44d" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa94cf79-04ea-5119-b7b2-32d4c3a82a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317386} {:answer/id #uuid "398108f0-8006-5cae-8c65-de61fbda6985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(-1,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317387} {:answer/id #uuid "f5530ecb-279e-5ecb-a53e-23aa9c0a3d68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }x\\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317388} {:answer/id #uuid "20538cc1-0f0d-5b32-aaaf-73462763337d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(1,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317389} {:answer/id #uuid "bd68a2a8-a3f0-5455-b025-7070a3c16cf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A parabola centered at $(1,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317390}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "398108f0-8006-5cae-8c65-de61fbda6985" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(-1,4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317387} :answer-field/id #uuid "d876644b-b2b3-5432-a6fb-95c01085e552" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317385}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9d23f0c9-2a90-5ec1-8032-cee754302a30" :question/math-academy-id "q-164350" :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(y-4)^{2}}{2}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(-1,4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbeda26-2fc0-5195-be26-ae79c72fc7bf" :knowledge-point/title "Traces of Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "723c343c-0f47-51d8-bb35-a3619d08f927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The hyperbola $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317393} {:answer/id #uuid "6826f8e0-8569-5f32-b740-21b9fdd97887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The line $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317394} {:answer/id #uuid "82963d38-17d9-5f5d-a422-6a77747d67c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $x=1$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317395} {:answer/id #uuid "89640ad3-319c-5b7c-9cbb-3d9cc87141f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The ellipse $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317396} {:answer/id #uuid "3348313e-e93b-53fe-950a-778491fc9c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The line $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82963d38-17d9-5f5d-a422-6a77747d67c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $x=1$ and $x=5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317395} :answer-field/id #uuid "31747716-6593-5f8a-8b90-395a6a655b06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3402cf6a-d8e4-5082-a8db-072c1ad24c36" :question/math-academy-id "q-164398" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{6}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(0)^{2}}{6} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{4} & =1 \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =3\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =1,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of two lines $x=1$ and $x=5$ in the $xy$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbeda26-2fc0-5195-be26-ae79c72fc7bf" :knowledge-point/title "Traces of Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3c1db30-d60a-53f3-b2a2-cdbb3c4e4898" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The parabola $(x+3)^{2}=6z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317400} {:answer/id #uuid "b538c52f-58f4-525c-90b0-595d1bce9948" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The line $y=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317401} {:answer/id #uuid "8a033994-7206-5d38-87a5-81db2249f53f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The parabola ${x}^{2}=6(z-3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317402} {:answer/id #uuid "af9dd4a1-d1b8-5217-b51b-6f766f3b34c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The line $y=\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317403} {:answer/id #uuid "ec1267fb-48d3-5536-8811-3e6dd8eb8aa7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The lines $y=-3$ and $y=15$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af9dd4a1-d1b8-5217-b51b-6f766f3b34c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The line $y=\\frac{3}{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317403} :answer-field/id #uuid "84a4f3de-bd74-5c02-a46d-c1fe74093a3a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c06a8e8-6e3d-50f3-ae2e-200f38471a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-164400" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the parabolic cylinder $(x+3)^{2}=6y?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $yz$-plane, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0+3)^{2} & =6y \\\\ 9 & =6y \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{6} \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a line in the $yz$-plane."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbeda26-2fc0-5195-be26-ae79c72fc7bf" :knowledge-point/title "Traces of Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "581a880f-0bee-593e-a0fe-021b2f89e73b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317407} {:answer/id #uuid "efd4100e-a77a-5b6d-8659-6d531d4fe79d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317408} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0aeada-f80b-5eac-a5c3-44173949397a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317409} {:answer/id #uuid "9771d26b-18a5-57ec-ab5c-803911edb87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317410} {:answer/id #uuid "172fd8c6-f2e5-5640-88ed-61a271db7548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba0aeada-f80b-5eac-a5c3-44173949397a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317409} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b90d15e-01af-5ee6-9eb1-1df6cf3b7bc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25bdc329-60cf-56f4-84fa-3414d97450cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-164411" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic cylinder $3(x+1)^{2}-6(z-2)^{2}=12$ and the plane $y=1$ onto the $xz$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the cylinder and the plane $y=1,$ we substitute $y=1$ into the equation of the cylinder. However, since the equation has no (explicit) $y$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(x+1)^{2}-6(z-2)^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{(2)^{2}}-\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $2$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbeda26-2fc0-5195-be26-ae79c72fc7bf" :knowledge-point/title "Traces of Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc6833a-3109-5cc5-955c-4b43c0b4fea8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317414} {:answer/id #uuid "170e12cf-bb71-5428-a64b-fbac5bb0e3f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{10}$ and $\\sqrt{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317415} {:answer/id #uuid "da31ce0b-ba99-547e-b9e8-883287d38071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317416} {:answer/id #uuid "d191ff0f-d416-506b-a474-c666f165354c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10$ and $5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317417} {:answer/id #uuid "2e8674ab-e315-5efe-bf50-c302f17a7768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2}$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317418}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da31ce0b-ba99-547e-b9e8-883287d38071" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317416} :answer-field/id #uuid "41d16978-9a2d-561d-933e-9c51c4124ec6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317413}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6fceebe4-a56d-5581-86c4-4cef01c078e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-164415" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the hyperbolic cylinder $2(x+6)^{2}-5{y}^{2}=10$ and the plane $z=5$ onto the $xy$-plane is a hyperbola. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the hyperbola?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the cylinder and the plane $z=5,$ we substitute $z=5$ into the equation of the cylinder. However, since the equation has no (explicit) $z$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+6)^{2}-5{y}^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{5}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+6)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of a hyperbola with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317419 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36c5224a-eccc-5ea9-8719-2b0dc2ddb44d" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dc5cc2fe-b608-5816-88d9-934c4687b7b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317421} {:answer/id #uuid "06a96e7c-b1b6-5224-8ad7-f72c5ce892d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(5,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317422} {:answer/id #uuid "3fdaf16d-20ed-552a-bb93-98f0dcbb06f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:xy=1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317423} {:answer/id #uuid "94c5abf4-3f1e-5b0f-aee3-7866c6972822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(-5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317424} {:answer/id #uuid "72f918f9-d6a9-52a1-945f-93828c686818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317425}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94c5abf4-3f1e-5b0f-aee3-7866c6972822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A hyperbola centered at $(-5,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317424} :answer-field/id #uuid "10cddbd1-e38d-5080-a329-091523cf8e5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a066ac1-6120-502f-9195-eb35e4541908" :question/math-academy-id "q-164417" :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the hyperbolic cylinder $(x+5)^{2}-{y}^{2}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x+5)^{2}-{y}^{2}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x+5)^{2}-{y}^{2}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(-5,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36c5224a-eccc-5ea9-8719-2b0dc2ddb44d" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a8dab05-7215-5d97-bb1f-bfc4eb25f09a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{R}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317428} {:answer/id #uuid "af5db50c-08a0-575c-8505-a142d1f66f59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An upward-opening parabola with a vertex at $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317429} {:answer/id #uuid "8436cad4-dc02-5eec-8e34-07d04a5352c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rightward-opening parabola with a vertex at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317430} {:answer/id #uuid "4fef373d-8acf-5f5a-850b-a54962d43554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An upward-opening parabola with a vertex at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317431} {:answer/id #uuid "846127f6-80c6-5475-8c62-e9d578d475d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rightward-opening parabola with a vertex at $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317432}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4fef373d-8acf-5f5a-850b-a54962d43554" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An upward-opening parabola with a vertex at $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317431} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ffdb7da-10a0-52b2-abdc-4f3aff6b8e03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317427}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8d66ef88-b3f7-5660-97bc-e01cb35d783f" :question/math-academy-id "q-164419" :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the parabolic cylinder $(x-1)^{2}=3y?$" :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our parabolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}=3y,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:(x-1)^{2}=3y}.\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola with a vertex at $(1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36c5224a-eccc-5ea9-8719-2b0dc2ddb44d" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc56ec28-a96b-5436-861a-734fc6daba5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(0,-3)$ with semi-axes of lengths $5$ and $3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317435} {:answer/id #uuid "310b9a52-f628-5be8-a91f-8f861feeb824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of an ellipse centered at $(0,-3)$ with semi-axes of lengths $25$ and $9.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317436} {:answer/id #uuid "28903c7a-d48c-56d5-acde-7e33685e8f88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(0,3)$ with semi-axes of lengths $5$ and $3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317437} {:answer/id #uuid "1cba55f4-8ff6-54a8-811e-3d09afc096ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(0,-3)$ with semi-axes of lengths $25$ and $9.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317438} {:answer/id #uuid "d2eafe95-d159-5ea8-ad17-714d737cb99d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of an ellipse centered at $(0,3)$ with semi-axes of lengths $5$ and $3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28903c7a-d48c-56d5-acde-7e33685e8f88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(0,3)$ with semi-axes of lengths $5$ and $3.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317437} :answer-field/id #uuid "74a5eb83-6dfb-5f51-b8c9-66242f24374c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317434}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1e07339-84d1-526a-a86d-71372b557ab9" :question/math-academy-id "q-164425" :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our elliptic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{9}=1}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-3)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse centered at $(0,3)$ with semi-axes of lengths $5$ and $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "abbeda26-2fc0-5195-be26-ae79c72fc7bf" :knowledge-point/title "Traces of Cylinders"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "896876a1-9e69-596f-9c61-8f62810fc6e4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121793} {:answer/id #uuid "8c6a72ce-482a-54c7-976f-b80d3ca276e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121794} {:answer/id #uuid "abc2db20-0450-5718-8f2f-c1d6f3aa8a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121795} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6e6581-507d-50e2-a396-732399b77ac3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121796} {:answer/id #uuid "754e90dc-cff5-535b-9f36-e0a5754f64c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "754e90dc-cff5-535b-9f36-e0a5754f64c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121797} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d200af-c807-53cb-9c53-d450e64afb6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fb4b9c0-1e83-54e2-ab98-0137b703d096" :question/math-academy-id "q-35363" :question/problem "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic cylinder $9{x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2}=18$ and the plane $z=1$ onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?" :question/worked-solution "To find the intersection of the cylinder and the plane $z=1,$ we substitute $z=1$ into the equation of the cylinder. However, since the equation has no (explicit) $z$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+2(y-1)^{2} & =18 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{(3)^{2}} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124808 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36c5224a-eccc-5ea9-8719-2b0dc2ddb44d" :knowledge-point/title "The Domain of a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4859138f-1589-596d-b406-fa33fd991478" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with semi-axes of lengths $2$ and $4.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317441} {:answer/id #uuid "359ba9ed-7b36-5bb2-a928-611685a4364d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with semi-axes of lengths $4$ and $2\\sqrt{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317442} {:answer/id #uuid "6bb4b6d0-fda0-5d48-a7f4-ecaae3a7ea4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of an ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with semi-axes of lengths $4$ and $2\\sqrt{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317443} {:answer/id #uuid "e78a8124-6354-59a5-b329-269bfa002c16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(-1,2)$ with semi-axes of lengths $2$ and $2\\sqrt{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317444} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9d9289-94ed-52d9-b20b-5182e7ef881d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The interior and boundary of an ellipse centered at $(1,2)$ with semi-axes of lengths $4$ and $2.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "359ba9ed-7b36-5bb2-a928-611685a4364d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with semi-axes of lengths $4$ and $2\\sqrt{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317442} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6730a19-ef34-5ca2-87f9-01479b0fd9e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc8b2a71-605f-561b-af0c-35843cefcc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-35569" :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our elliptic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}=1}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=1}.\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with semi-axes of lengths $4$ and $2\\sqrt{2}.$"}]}
